  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does the steam need to be at high pressure?",
      "a": "High pressure and temperature pack more energy into each kilogram of steam, and the big drop down to the condenser gives the steam more to push with. Power station steam can be at more than 160 times normal air pressure.",
      "asks": [
        "How strong is the steam in a turbine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do turbine blades get bigger along the shaft?",
      "a": "As steam passes each row it loses pressure and swells to fill much more space. Longer blades give the growing volume of steam room to pass while it keeps pushing the shaft round.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are the last turbine blades the biggest?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How is a turbine's speed controlled?",
      "a": "Control valves let more or less steam in. On a 50 hertz grid a big generator turns at exactly 3,000 revolutions per minute, and the valves open further when more electricity is needed.",
      "asks": [
        "What keeps a turbine spinning at the right speed?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are there many rows of blades instead of one?",
      "a": "Taking the energy out in many small steps is much more efficient than one huge blast, which would spin a single wheel impossibly fast. Big turbines have dozens of rows, split into high, intermediate and low pressure sections.",
      "asks": [
        "What are turbine stages?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the condenser do for a turbine?",
      "a": "It turns the exhaust steam back into water, which leaves a near vacuum behind the last blades. That low pressure lets the steam expand further and do more work on the way.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is there a vacuum after a turbine?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Charles Parsons invented the modern multi-stage steam turbine in 1884.",
    "In 1897 the turbine-powered boat Turbinia reached about 34 knots, faster than any other ship of its time.",
    "Last-stage blades in large power station turbines can be over 1.5 metres long."
  ],
